=== (A) Substitute m = lambda*sqrt(n), n = 1/eps^2 (eps=1/sqrt(n)->0) ===
A(eps) [before series] has 622 chars; extracting leading order...
A(eps), g'''(eps), g''''(eps) Puiseux series computed (each 1264 / 2165 / 3404 chars -- full expressions in this run's stdout capture, elided here
for readability; only the leading term of each is used below).

=== (B) Extracting the leading power of eps in each, symbolically ===
A        ~ (gamma**2/lambda) * eps^(-3)   i.e. order n^3/2 = n^3/2
g'''(t*) ~ (2*gamma**3/lambda**2) * eps^(-4)   i.e. order n^2
g''''(t*)~ (-6*gamma**4/lambda**3) * eps^(-5)   i.e. order n^5/2

=== (C) Delta = g''''(t*)/(8A^2) + 5[g'''(t*)]^2/(24A^3): leading order ===
g''''(t*)/(8A^2)        leading eps-power: 1  (n^-1/2)
5[g'''(t*)]^2/(24A^3)   leading eps-power: 1  (n^-1/2)

=== (C') Leading-order Delta computed directly from the already-
     extracted leading coefficients (cA,pA),(c3,p3),(c4,p4) ===
term A (g''''/(8A^2)):  coeff = -3/(4*lambda)  power = 1
term B (5g'''^2/(24A^3)): coeff = 5/(6*lambda)  power = 1
Both terms share leading eps-power 1 -- summing coefficients (no cancellation to zero):
  cD = 1/(12*lambda)  (nonzero: confirms no leading-order cancellation between the two Delta pieces)

Delta ~ (1/(12*lambda)) * eps^(1)  =  c(lambda,gamma) * n^(-1/2)
c(lambda,gamma) closed form: 1/(12*lambda)

